{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_32","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,220p' /home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/code/combo_new/seed1315637744/10/codegen_prompts/2.md\"","aggregated_output":"你要为一个新的 ARC-AGI 风格题目实现代码。代码文件必须包含 `main(grid: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray` 和 `generate_input() -> np.ndarray`。\n\n## 新题位置\n\n- 描述目录：`/home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed1315637744/10`\n- 题号：`2`\n- 目标代码路径：`/home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/code/combo_new/seed1315637744/10/2.py`\n\n## 新题描述\n\n<new_puzzle index=\"2\">\n- `description`：将散布的彩色碎片按颜色归组，每组按面积从小到大排序后，依次嵌套堆叠成同心环形图案。\n- `Input structure`：背景为 azure。网格中散布着若干组彩色 4-连通对象，每组使用一种独特颜色（如 red、green、yellow、magenta），每种颜色恰好有 3 个不同面积的实心矩形对象。网格的一个角落有一个由 grey 像素标记的小十字（+形），指示输出图案的中心位置。\n- `Rule`：第一步：将同色的三个矩形按面积从大到小排序，记为 L、M、S。第二步：依据颜色在网格中出现的从上到下、从左到右的首次出现顺序排列各颜色组。第三步：以 grey 十字的中心为锚点，将第一组颜色的 L 居中放置，然后在其正中央叠放第一组的 M，再叠放 S；接着对第二组颜色做同样操作——第二组的 L 居中覆盖在第一组 S 之上，依此类推。最终形成多层同心矩形嵌套，最外层是第一组的 L，最内层是最后一组的 S。输出网格裁剪为恰好包含整个嵌套图案的最小矩形。\n- `Key insight`：解题者必须先发现\"同色归组 + 面积排序\"的分组逻辑，再发现\"颜色组之间按空间首现顺序决定嵌套层级\"的第二层规则，最后理解居中叠放的几何操作。三层推理环环相扣。\n</new_puzzle>\n\n## 机制相似参考题\n\n下面参考题由本地相似度流程自动选择：先用 `annotations`/docstring/代码特征抽取机制标签，再按机制相似度、文本相似度和代码特征相似度重排，并用 MMR 做去重。优先学习这些题的对象识别方式、随机生成器约束、边界条件处理，不要照搬旧题规则。\n\n已选参考：train/6cbe9eb8, train/652646ff, train/e3fe1151, train/58c02a16, eval/a251c730, eval/58490d8a, train/d749d46f, train/57edb29d\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"1\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"6cbe9eb8\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.4261\nmechanism=0.5169\nlexical=0.0130\ncode_feature=0.6000\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:connected_components, code:output_resize, object:rectangle, object:sprite, operation:count, operation:crop_resize, operation:sort_order, output:crop, relation:direction\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/train/6cbe9eb8.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/6cbe9eb8.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nRebuild the foreground rectangles as a largest-to-smallest lower-left nesting.\n\nInput structure\n- A periodic wallpaper background is overlaid by foreground colors that each form a rectangular frame or solid rectangle.\n- The largest foreground rectangle is the output frame; smaller foreground rectangles are the objects to nest inside it.\n- A frame may be partially hidden by a smaller rectangle, but its visible pixels still determine its bbox.\n\nRule\n- Ignore the wallpaper and crop the result to the largest foreground rectangle's bbox size.\n- Sort foreground rectangles by bbox area from largest to smallest.\n- Reconstruct each frame or solid rectangle, placing every next smaller rectangle in the lower-left of the current rectangle's usable region.\n\nKey insight\n- The scattered background colors are not objects; the meaningful order is the unique descending rectangle size chain, and each step nests into the previous rectangle's lower-left interior.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n  \"type\": \"move_in_order\",\n  \"src\": \"foreground rectangular frames and solid rectangles over periodic wallpaper\",\n  \"dst\": \"nested lower-left inside the largest foreground rectangle\",\n  \"transform\": \"discard the wallpaper; sort rectangles by bbox area descending; rebuild each frame or solid rectangle, placing each next smaller rectangle at the lower-left of the current frame interior or solid bbox\",\n  \"grid_shape\": \"crop to the largest foreground rectangle bbox\"\n}\n</annotation>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"2\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"652646ff\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.4231\nmechanism=0.5481\nlexical=0.0121\ncode_feature=0.4000\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:output_resize, object:marker, object:sprite, operation:count, operation:crop_resize, operation:sort_order, output:crop, relation:direction, relation:overlap, relation:row_col\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/train/652646ff.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/652646ff.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nRecover each partially hidden 6x6 ring sprite and stack complete copies from top layer to bottom layer.\n\nInput structure:\n- The dominant color is the background.\n- Several non-background colors are visible fragments of the same 12-pixel\n  outline ring in a 6x6 box; small off-template marker pixels are noise.\n- Ring fragments overlap, so upper rings replace some expected pixels of\n  lower rings, and a ring may also be clipped by the input boundary.\n\nRule:\n- Identify every substantial color whose pixels all lie on one translated\n  copy of the ring template.\n- For each missing in-bounds template pixel, the color currently occupying\n  that pixel is above the hidden ring.\n- Topologically sort these occlusion relations from top to bottom, then draw\n  one complete 6x6 ring for each color in that order on a background canvas.\n\nKey insight:\n- The output is not a crop of the scene; it is the layer stack of complete\n  ring sprites reconstructed from their visible fragments.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n  \"type\": \"move_in_order\",\n  \"src\": \"visible fragments of overlapping 6x6 outline-ring sprites; sparse off-template colors are marker noise\",\n  \"dst\": \"vertical stack of one completed ring sprite per recovered color\",\n  \"transform\": \"complete every fragment to the 12-pixel ring template using the input background\",\n  \"multi_step\": \"order sprites from top layer to bottom layer: if a color occupies another sprite's expected ring pixel, it is placed earlier\",\n  \"grid_shape\": \"6 columns; height is 6 times the number of recovered ring sprites\"\n}\n</annotation>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"3\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"e3fe1151\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.3738\nmechanism=0.4399\nlexical=0.0135\ncode_feature=0.6000\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:counter, code:output_resize, object:marker, object:rectangle, operation:count, operation:sort_order, relation:row_col\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/train/e3fe1151.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/e3fe1151.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nFill the one blank in each corner region so all four regions have the same color multiset.\n\nInput structure:\n- The dominant background color forms one solid separator row and one solid\n  separator column.\n- The separator cross splits the grid into four equal rectangular corner regions.\n- Each corner region contains exactly one background-colored blank pixel.\n\nRule:\n- Count the non-background colors in each corner region.\n- The completed region multiset is defined by the maximum observed count of\n  each color across the four regions.\n- Replace each region's blank pixel with the single color needed to match that\n  completed multiset.\n\nKey insight:\n- The separator color inside a corner region is a missing value marker; comparing\n  the four regions reveals the shared multiset without using numeric color order.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n  \"type\": \"transform_in_place\",\n  \"src\": \"four equal corner regions divided by a solid background cross, each with one background-colored blank pixel\",\n  \"transform\": \"fill each blank with the color needed so every corner region has the same color multiset, using the maximum observed count of each color across the regions\"\n}\n</annotation>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"4\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"58c02a16\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.3925\nmechanism=0.5027\nlexical=0.0133\ncode_feature=0.4000\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:output_resize, object:marker, object:rectangle, object:sprite, operation:crop_resize, operation:sort_order, output:crop, relation:direction, relation:row_col\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/train/58c02a16.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/58c02a16.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nExpand the top-left seed mask into a recursive colored pattern and tile it across the full grid.\n\nInput structure\n- The background is the dominant color.\n- All foreground lies in a top-left rectangle whose last row and last column are a solid secondary color.\n- Removing that secondary-color row and column leaves a seed region containing background plus one primary color.\n\nRule\n- Use the primary-color pixels in the seed as a binary mask.\n- Replace each primary seed cell with a copy of the seed mask in the primary color, and each background seed cell\n  with a copy of the seed mask in the secondary color.\n- Repeat and crop that expanded tile to the original grid shape.\n\nKey insight\nThe solid L-shaped secondary-color border is a legend, not output content; it supplies the color used when the\nseed's background cells are expanded.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n  \"type\": \"symmetry/pattern\",\n  \"src\": \"top-left seed region containing background and one primary color, with a solid secondary-color L-shaped legend border\",\n  \"transform\": \"expand the seed mask once: primary seed cells become primary-color copies of the mask, background seed cells become secondary-color copies, and mask background stays background; tile/crop the expanded pattern to the full grid\"\n}\n</annotation>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"5\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"a251c730\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.4059\nmechanism=0.4753\nlexical=0.0136\ncode_feature=0.6667\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:connected_components, code:counter, code:output_resize, object:marker, object:rectangle, object:sprite, operation:crop_resize, operation:sort_order, output:crop, relation:overlap\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/eval/a251c730.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/a251c730.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nStamp source sprites into a destination frame via color-matched singleton markers.\n\nInput structure:\n- Two bordered rectangles: source (multi-cell sprites) and destination (singleton markers).\n- Each sprite has an anchor pixel — its least-frequent color. Markers share these colors.\n\nRule:\n- Match each destination marker to the source sprite with the same anchor color.\n- Translate the sprite so its anchor overlaps the marker position.\n- Output is the destination rectangle, interior cleared before stamping.\n\nKey insight: The rarest color per sprite doubles as a template key and positioning anchor.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n  \"type\": \"move_to_match\",\n  \"src\": \"sprite s with anchor pixel inside the source framed rectangle\",\n  \"dst\": \"anchored on singleton marker pixel d inside the destination framed rectangle\",\n  \"match_cond\": \"s.anchor_color == d.color\",\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
